1
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:46,000
A world where heroes come in all sizes and
adventure is the greatest magic of all.

2
00:09:20,000 --> 00:09:26,000
Willow is a fantasy, adventure, action film directed by Ron Howard.

3
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:06,000
The evil Queen Bavmorda hunts the newborn princess Elora Danan,
a child prophesied to bring about her downfall.

4
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:46,000
When the royal infant is found by Willow, a timid farmer and aspiring
sorcerer, he's entrusted with delivering her from evil.

5
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:26,000
Madmartigan, played by Val Kilmer.

6
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:06,000
Sorsha, played by Joanne Whalley.

7
00:32:40,000 --> 00:32:46,000
Willow, played by Warwick Davis.

8
00:37:20,000 --> 00:37:26,000
Raziel, played by Patricia Hayes.

9
00:42:00,000 --> 00:42:06,000
Airk, played by Gavan O'Herlihy.

10
00:46:40,000 --> 00:46:46,000
Vohnkar, played by Phil Fondacaro.

11
00:51:20,000 --> 00:51:26,000
Willow is a 1988 American high fantasy adventure film
directed by Ron Howard and produced by Nigel Wooll.

12
00:56:00,000 --> 00:56:06,000
The film was executive produced by George Lucas and
written by Bob Dolman from a story by Lucas.

13
01:00:40,000 --> 01:00:46,000
The film stars Warwick Davis as the title character, Val
Kilmer, Joanne Whalley, Billy Barty and Jean Marsh.

14
01:05:20,000 --> 01:05:26,000
Davis portrays aspiring magician Willow Ufgood who teams up with disaffected warrior Madmartigan
(Kilmer) to protect young baby princess Elora Danan from evil queen Bavmorda (Marsh).

15
01:10:00,000 --> 01:10:06,000
Lucas conceived the idea for the film in 1972, approaching Howard to
direct during the post-production phase of Cocoon in 1985.

16
01:14:40,000 --> 01:14:46,000
Bob Dolman was brought in to write the screenplay, coming up with seven
drafts that Lucas was actively involved in developing.

17
01:19:20,000 --> 01:19:26,000
It was finished in late 1986.

18
01:24:00,000 --> 01:24:06,000
It was then set up at Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer (MGM) and principal
photography began in April 1987, finishing the following October.

19
01:28:40,000 --> 01:28:46,000
The majority of filming took place in Dinorwic quarry in Wales with some at Elstree
Studios in Hertfordshire, as well as a small section in New Zealand.

20
01:33:20,000 --> 01:33:26,000
Industrial Light & Magic created the visual effects and animation sequences, which
led to a revolutionary breakthrough with digital morphing technology.

21
01:38:00,000 --> 01:38:06,000
The film was released in 1988 to mixed reviews from critics with praise for the visual effects, humour, character
designs and Warwick Davis's performances but criticism for its direction, pacing, tone and story.

22
01:42:40,000 --> 01:42:46,000
It grossed $137.6 million worldwide against a $35 million budget.

23
01:47:20,000 --> 01:47:26,000
While not the blockbuster some expected, it turned a profit based on international
box office returns and strong home video and television returns.

24
01:52:00,000 --> 01:52:06,000
It received two Academy Award nominations.

25
01:56:40,000 --> 01:56:46,000
A television series that served as a sequel to the
film was released on Disney+ in 2022.

26
02:01:20,000 --> 02:01:26,000
The film explores themes of mission, hero, baby, monster, magic.
